{%- comment -%}
  Icon for #ka-rail (layout/theme.liquid), repeated vertically down the
  rail. Each of the 13 cases below is one letterform from Santosh's
  per-script "KCC Letters - <script>.svg" exports (Bengali, English "Ka",
  Gujarati, Hindi, Kannada, Malayalam, Meitei, Odia, Ol Chiki, Punjabi,
  Tamil, Telugu, Urdu) - viewBox/path data taken verbatim from those
  files, with the viewBox tightened to each glyph's real rendered
  bounding box via a headless-browser getBBox() pass (not hand-measured).

  Each <svg>'s width/height (set inline below, not in the shared CSS
  rule) sizes it so its own aspect ratio fills that box - i.e. no
  letterboxing - at a shared 566px^2 target area (box_w = sqrt(area x
  aspect), box_h = sqrt(area / aspect)), rather than every glyph sharing
  one fixed square. Santosh flagged that fitting every glyph's longest
  side into an identical box (the old approach) was making some scripts
  look visibly smaller than others - a wide viewBox and a tall one both
  get letterboxed down to fit a square, but by very different amounts.
  Sizing by area instead keeps that fairer across very different native
  proportions. 566px^2 = 784px^2 (the box this started from) x 0.85^2 -
  Santosh asked for the glyphs 15% smaller once the equal-area sizing
  read correctly; scaling both box dimensions by 0.85 is what "15%
  smaller" means for a 2D shape (dropping the area itself by 0.85 would
  only be a ~7.7% linear shrink) and leaves the equal-area balance
  between glyphs untouched. See the matching comment on #ka-rail
  .ka-rail__icons in layout/theme.liquid for why this also means the
  scroll animation's loop distance is no longer a clean per-icon
  multiple - recompute both together (and the gap there) if any glyph
  here changes.

  English and Ol Chiki are the two two-part cases - both files draw their
  "Ka" as two separate paths (two letters for English; two Ol Chiki
  characters for the syllable), kept together in a single icon each (one
  shared viewBox spanning both parts, via getBBox() on their union) so
  the rail reads a whole word rather than splitting across two rotation
  slots. Meitei's syllable is a single character, so it's one path.

  Meitei and Ol Chiki were previously excluded: Santosh's first export of
  those two carried the letterform as an embedded base64 PNG rather than
  a vector path, and both PNGs had a truncated IDAT chunk. His re-export
  is proper vector paths (fill + construction-reference stroke outlines,
  serif:id="_Image..." raster gone entirely) - only the filled paths are
  used here, the redundant stroke-only outline paths are dropped. Their
  fill-rule (evenodd, per the source files' root <svg style>) was checked
  against nonzero via a rendered comparison - both produce an identical
  result for these particular shapes, so nonzero was kept for consistency
  with every other case here.

  Urdu's file also carries a second, decorative raster element (a wide
  flourish) alongside its vector letterform - that raster is corrupted,
  so only the vector path is used here; the flourish is dropped.

  fill="currentColor" on each path so #ka-rail's own color setting
  controls it, same as before.

  @param {number} index - which glyph to render, 0-12
{%- endcomment -%}
